{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"BT755 .K54"},"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Catholic University of America. Studies in sacred theology, 2d ser. ; no. 115"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Necessity of the church for salvation in selected theological writings of the past"},"name":{"namePart":"King, John J."},"originInfo":{"publisher":"Washington : Catholic University of America Press, 1960"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)610482683"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Studies in sacred theology 2nd series, no. 115"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990025810270203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":{"@type":"oclc","#text":"10193538"},"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000121705139","#text":"Harvard Divinity School Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"684 K53n"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"C 10619.2 vol.115"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":["Divinity Library","Widener"]},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990025810270203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"002581027"}},"processingDate":"2023-09-29T05:13Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"IDJ"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"831206"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20020606"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990025810270203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.6 using MARC21slim2MODS3-6.xsl\n        (Revision 1.117 2017/02/14)"}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"Writing women in Jacobean England"},"name":{"@nameTitleGroup":"1","@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Lewalski, Barbara Kiefer",{"@type":"date","#text":"1931-2018"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Criticism, interpretation, etc."},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"mau"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Massachusetts"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Cambridge, Mass"}}],"publisher":"Harvard University Press","dateIssued":"1993","issuance":"monographic"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xii, 431 p. : ill. ; 25 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"When was feminism born - in the 1960s, or in the 1660s? For England, one might answer: the early decades of the seventeenth century. James I was King of England, and women were expected to be chaste, obedient, subordinate, and silent. Some, however, were not, and these are the women who interest Barbara Lewalski - those who, as queens and petitioners, patrons and historians and poets, took up the pen to challenge and subvert the repressive patriarchal ideology of Jacobean England. Setting out to show how these women wrote themselves into their culture, Lewalski rewrites Renaissance history to include some of its most compelling - and neglected - voices. As a culture dominated by a powerful Queen gave way to the rule of a patriarchal ideologue, a woman's subjection to father and husband came to symbolize the subjection of all English people to their monarch, and all Christians to God. Remarkably enough, it is in this repressive Jacobean milieu that we first hear Englishwomen's own voices in some number. Elizabeth Cary, Aemilia Lanyer, Rachel Speght, and Mary Wroth published original poems, dramas, and prose of considerable scope and merit; others inscribed their thoughts and experiences in letters and memoirs. Queen Anne used the court masque to assert her place in palace politics, while Princess Elizabeth herself stood as a symbol of resistance to Jacobean patriarchy. By looking at these women through their works, Lewalski documents the flourishing of a sense of feminine identity and expression in spite of - or perhaps because of - the constraints of the time. The result is a fascinating sampling of Jacobean women's lives and works, restored to their rightful place in literary history and cultural politics. In these women's voices and perspectives, Lewalski identifies an early challenge to the dominant culture - and an ongoing challenge to our understanding of the Renaissance world."},"tableOfContents":"Introduction: Women, writing, and resistance in Jacobean England. I. The royal opposition. 1. Enacting opposition: Queen Anne and the subversions of masquing. 2. Scripting a heroine's role: Princess Elizabeth and the politics of romance. 3. Writing resistance in letters: Arbella Stuart and the rhetoric of disguise and defiance. II. Re-writing patriarchy. 4. Exercising power: the Countess of Bedford as courtier, patron, and coterie poet. 5. Claiming patrimony and constructing a self: Anne Clifford and her Diary. 6. Defending women's essential equality: Rachel Speght's polemics and poems. III. Literary re-visions. 7. Resisting tyrants: Elizabeth Cary's tragedy and history. 8. Imagining female community: Aemilia Lanyer's poems. 9. Revising genres and claiming the woman's part: Mary Wroth's oeuvre. Afterword: The politics of Jacobean women's writing. Appendix A: Elizabeth, Lady Falkland, and the authorship of Edward II. Appendix B: Presentation copies of Lanyer's Salve Deus rex judaeorum.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Barbara Kiefer Lewalski."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 323-411) and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":[{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-uk-en"},{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-uk---"}]},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Anne",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Queen, consort of James I, King of England"},{"@type":"date","#text":"1574-1619"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Elizabeth",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Queen, consort of Frederick I, King of Bohemia"},{"@type":"date","#text":"1596-1662"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Stuart, Arabella",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Lady"},{"@type":"date","#text":"1575-1615"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Russell, Lucy Harington",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Countess of Bedford"},{"@type":"date","#text":"1581-1627"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Pembroke, Anne Clifford Herbert",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Countess of"},{"@type":"date","#text":"1590-1676"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Speght, Rachel"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Cary, Elizabeth",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Lady"},{"@type":"date","#text":"1585 or 1586-1639"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Lanyer, Aemilia"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Wroth, Mary",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Lady"},{"@type":"date","#text":"approximately 1586-approximately 1640"}]}},{"temporal":"Geschichte 1603-1625"},{"@authority":"fast","temporal":"1500-1700"},{"@authority":"swd","temporal":"Geschichte 1603-1625"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["English literature","History and criticism"],"temporal":"Early modern, 1500-1700"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["English lite